      "Venice sits in a lagoon on the Adriatic coast of north-eastern Italy, made up of over a hundred small islands linked by more than 400 bridges, with a historic-centre population of about 47,600 (end of 2025, now below 50,000 and still declining, and the outflow of residents is a long-running local issue). It was one of the great medieval Mediterranean trading powers, and its canals and car-free layout are its most distinctive feature; the whole Venetian Lagoon is a World Heritage Site.",
    // howItWorks sources: Wikipedia (2026-09): Venice (comune = historic city + lagoon + Mestre/Marghera merged 1926; 6 municipalità; ~250,000 2024, historic centre ~48,000; 6 sestieri numbering; ACTV; Ponte della Libertà 1933; UNESCO 1987); Comune
    howItWorks:
      "The Venice people speak of is, administratively, a commune far larger than the water city: it takes in the historic centre in the lagoon, islands such as Murano and Burano, and the mainland districts of Mestre and Marghera merged in 1926, divided into six boroughs (municipalità) and governed by a mayor and council. The commune had about 251,000 people at the end of 2025, of whom only about 47,600 live in the historic centre, a number falling every year; most residents live on the mainland and cross daily by the Ponte della Libertà of 1933, with Piazzale Roma as the last point reachable by car or bus. The historic centre is divided into six sestieri, and house numbers run continuously within each sestiere rather than by street, so one number may be several alleys from the next. The vaporetto water buses run by ACTV are the only public transport on the islands, and the airport is on the mainland. Venice and its lagoon were inscribed as World Heritage in 1987, and since 2024 an access fee has been trialled for day visitors. Newcomers most often go wrong by taking Venice for the water city alone, when its town hall, most of its residents and its airport are on the mainland.",
